//! `Float` <-> text: shortest round-trip formatting and correctly-rounded
//! parsing, both in pure Saw (design 253).
//!
//! ```saw
//! print(0.1 + 0.2)                  // 0.30000000000000004
//! let back = "0.30000000000000004".to_float()!
//! print(back == 0.1 + 0.2)          // true
//! ```
//!
//! One module owns the whole story, in both directions, and every surface in
//! it funnels through one routine per direction:
//!
//!   * `StringBuilder.append(value: Float)` is THE formatter. `Float.to_string`
//!     calls it, and so does every rendering the compiler emits — `print(f)`,
//!     `"{f}"`, `print("{}", f)`, a `panic`/`assert` argument, and
//!     `f.format(into:)` — through `_value_to_string`'s FLOAT arm in
//!     `sawc/codegen/core.py`. There is no second float renderer anywhere.
//!   * `String.to_float` is THE parser, and `std.json` reads its numbers
//!     through it.
//!
//! It is declared here rather than in `std.string` / `std.stringbuilder`
//! because the two directions share the bit-level layer below, and splitting
//! them would mean inventing a second public spelling for each just to cross
//! a file boundary. Both of those files carry a pointer comment saying so.
//!
//! **The text is exactly what a shortest round-trip is:** the produced decimal
//! parses back to the identical bits, and no shorter decimal does. The layout
//! is CPython's `repr` — see `StringBuilder.append(value: Float)` for the rule
//! written out. `nan` / `inf` / `-inf` are the non-finite spellings; those are
//! Saw-facing text and NOT JSON, whose own rules are in `sawc/std/JSON.md`.
//!
//! Allocation: the formatter writes bytes through `StringBuilder.append`, so a
//! fixed builder renders a float with no allocator at all — which is what lets
//! a `panic` message name one. `Float.to_string` allocates exactly once, for
//! the `String` it returns.
//!
//! Freestanding: nothing here calls libc. The algorithms are integer-only and
//! the tables are read-only data.
//!
//! ## The algorithms
//!
//! Formatting is **Ryu** (Ulf Adams, PLDI 2018, "Ryū: fast float-to-string
//! conversion") — its `d2d` shortest-decimal core, transcribed from the
//! reference C implementation's `d2s.c`, with the two 128-bit power-of-five
//! tables generated by `tools/sawfloat.py tables`.
//!
//! Parsing is **Eisel-Lemire** (Daniel Lemire, "Number Parsing at a Gigabyte
//! per Second", SPE 2021; after Michael Eisel) for the fast path, falling back
//! to the **simple decimal conversion** of Clinger/Steele-White — a decimal
//! digit buffer shifted by powers of two, which is exact by construction — for
//! the inputs the fast path declines. See `String.to_float`.

// =============================================================================
// Shape constants
// =============================================================================

// IEEE 754 binary64, which is what `Float` is (LANGUAGE_SPEC: "the one float
// type"). Named rather than spelled inline, per the no-magic-numbers rule.
static FLOAT_MANTISSA_BITS: Int = 52
static FLOAT_EXPONENT_BITS: Int = 11
static FLOAT_EXPONENT_BIAS: Int = 1023
static FLOAT_EXPONENT_ALL_ONES: Int = 2047
static FLOAT_MANTISSA_MASK: UInt64 = 0x000F_FFFF_FFFF_FFFF
static FLOAT_EXPONENT_MASK: UInt64 = 0x0000_0000_0000_07FF
static FLOAT_SIGN_MASK: UInt64 = 0x8000_0000_0000_0000
static FLOAT_IMPLICIT_BIT: UInt64 = 0x0010_0000_0000_0000

// Ryu's two table scalings. Both tables hold 128-bit values as (low, high)
// pairs, so entry `i` is at `2 * i` and `2 * i + 1`.
static FLOAT_POW5_INV_BITCOUNT: Int = 125
static FLOAT_POW5_BITCOUNT: Int = 125

// The most significant digits a shortest round-trip decimal can need, and the
// widest rendering one can produce. The longest text is the exponent form of a
// 17-digit negative value with a three-digit exponent —
// `-1.2345678901234567e-308` — which is 24 bytes.
static FLOAT_MAX_DIGITS: Int = 17
static FLOAT_TEXT_MAX: Int = 24

// ASCII the renderer writes. `Byte`, so `StringBuilder.append` reaches the
// raw-byte overload rather than the integer-rendering one (design 250 §4).
static FLOAT_BYTE_MINUS: Byte = 45
static FLOAT_BYTE_PLUS: Byte = 43
static FLOAT_BYTE_DOT: Byte = 46
static FLOAT_BYTE_ZERO: Byte = 48
static FLOAT_BYTE_E: Byte = 101

// The exponent form's switch, which is `repr`'s: a value whose decimal point
// sits more than four places left of the digits, or more than sixteen places
// right of their start, renders as `d.ddde±dd`.
static FLOAT_FIXED_LOW: Int = -4
static FLOAT_FIXED_HIGH: Int = 16

// BEGIN GENERATED TABLES — regenerate with `tools/sawfloat.py tables`

static FLOAT_POW5_INV_SPLIT: [UInt64; 584] = [
